type Option = { id: string; label: string };
type Props = {
  value: string;
  onChange: (value: string) => void;
  search: (query: string) => Promise<Option[]>;
};

function CityPicker({ value, onChange, search }: Props) {
  const [options, setOptions] = useState<Option[]>([]);
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [active, setActive] = useState(0);

  async function handleChange(next: string) {
    onChange(next);
    setOpen(true);
    const results = await search(next);   // no debounce, no abort, no owner
    setOptions(results);
    setActive(0);
  }

  function onKeyDown(e: React.KeyboardEvent) {
    if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      e.preventDefault();
      setActive(i => i + 1);
    }
    if (e.key === 'Enter') {
      const picked = options[active];
      if (picked) { onChange(picked.label); setOpen(false); }
    }
  }

  return (
    <>
      <input
        aria-label='City'
        value={value}
        onChange={e => handleChange(e.target.value)}
        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
      />
      {open && (
        <ul role='listbox'>
          {options.map((option, i) => (
            <li
              key={option.id}
              role='option'
              aria-selected={i === active}
              onMouseDown={() => { onChange(option.label); setOpen(false); }}
            >
              {option.label}
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
I would name the four mechanisms before touching the code, because each has its own fix. The picker writes state from whatever response resolves last, so an older request can land after a newer one. It fires one request per keystroke, because nothing debounces and nothing aborts. It looks like a combobox but exposes none of it: the input has a name and no `role=combobox`, no `aria-expanded`, no `aria-controls`, no `aria-activedescendant`, and the highlight is an array index. And a mouse pick blurs the input, because the option is a non-focusable `<li>` and the handler does not prevent the default mousedown.

The request lifecycle is where the race dies. The first contract change is `search(query, signal)`, because an API that cannot be aborted can only be ignored. Then one effect keyed on the value owns the whole lifecycle, with the timer and the controller cleaned up:

```tsx
const latest = useRef(0);
const committed = useRef<string | null>(null);

useEffect(() => {
  const query = value.trim();
  if (query.length < 2 || value === committed.current) {
    setState({ kind: 'idle' });
    setOpen(false);
    return;
  }
  const id = ++latest.current;                 // this request's owner token
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
    search(query, controller.signal)
      .then(options => {
        if (id !== latest.current) return;     // a newer query already owns the list
        setState({ kind: 'success', options });
        setActiveId(options[0]?.id ?? null);
        setOpen(true);
      })
      .catch((error: unknown) => {
        if (controller.signal.aborted || id !== latest.current) return;  // expected
        setState({ kind: 'error', message: 'Search failed. Try again.' });
      });
  }, 250);
  return () => { clearTimeout(timer); controller.abort(); };
}, [value]);
```

The abort is the cheap half: it stops work nobody wants. The token is the half that makes the screen correct, because a response can already be in flight when the abort fires, so the id check, not the abort, decides who may write to state. `committed` stops the effect from searching for the label I just wrote; the input's own `onChange` clears it before reporting the new value.

The ARIA side is a fixed wiring once the shape is right: the input is the combobox, so it carries `role=combobox`, `aria-expanded={open}`, `aria-controls={listId}` while open, and `aria-activedescendant` pointing at the active option's id; the popup is a listbox of options with real roles and ids, and options never take `tabindex`, because Tab has to leave the field.

Selection state moves from an index to an id. An index into `options` is meaningless the moment a new array replaces it — that is why the highlight can end up naming a different city — and it needs clamping at both ends, which `setActive(i => i + 1)` never does:

```tsx
const activeIndex = options.findIndex(o => o.id === activeId);
function move(delta: number) {
  if (!options.length) return;
  const next = activeIndex < 0 ? 0
    : Math.min(options.length - 1, Math.max(0, activeIndex + delta));
  setActiveId(options[next].id);
}
function commit(option: Option) {
  onChange(option.label);
  committed.current = option.label;
  setOpen(false);
  setActiveId(null);
}
```

The mouse path uses `onMouseDown` with `e.preventDefault()` and the same `commit`, so the input never loses focus and the keyboard continues where it was. Escape closes without touching the value, ArrowUp mirrors ArrowDown, Enter commits only when something is active, and loading, empty and error states render from a discriminated union instead of being inferred from `options.length`. With the remaining time I would add `aria-autocomplete=list`, a minimum-query hint, and a test that resolves the two requests out of order.

