type ModalProps = {
  open: boolean;
  titleId: string;
  onClose: () => void;
  children: React.ReactNode;
};

function Modal({ open, titleId, onClose, children }: ModalProps) {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (open) ref.current?.focus();
  }, [open]);

  if (!open) return null;

  return (
    <div
      ref={ref}
      role='dialog'
      aria-modal='true'
      aria-labelledby={titleId}
      tabIndex={-1}
      className='overlay'
      onKeyDown={e => {
        if (e.target === e.currentTarget && e.key === 'Escape') onClose();
      }}
    >
      <div className='panel'>{children}</div>
      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
    </div>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
The code does exactly four things: it mounts an overlay with `role=dialog`, `aria-modal=true` and the label reference; it moves focus to that overlay, a `tabIndex={-1}` div, after mount; it closes when Escape arrives with the overlay itself as the event target; and it unmounts on close. The modality it asserts is not among them.

Symptom by symptom. Tab walks out because nothing contains it — the page behind is still in the tab order, so the browser's natural order takes focus from Close to whatever comes next in the document. A screen reader reads the product list because `aria-modal=true` is a claim, not a mechanism: it tells assistive tech that content outside the dialog is unavailable, while changing nothing in the DOM. The background stays in the tab order and stays exposed, so where the rest of the page was not actually made inert the attribute is false and produces exactly the browse-mode leak QA saw. Focus lands on `<body>` after close because nothing captured the opener: the focused node is removed and the browser has nowhere to return to. Escape stops working on the Close button because of `e.target === e.currentTarget` — the handler sits on the overlay and keydown from a child bubbles with `target` set to the child, so the guard excludes the case where the user has tabbed into the dialog.

The version I would merge is the native one, because the browser already implements everything being claimed:

```tsx
function Modal({ open, titleId, onClose, children }: ModalProps) {
  const dialogRef = useRef<HTMLDialogElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const dialog = dialogRef.current;
    if (!dialog) return;

    function handleClose() { onClose(); }
    function handleCancel(e: Event) {
      if (!isDirty()) return;          // let the browser dismiss it
      e.preventDefault();              // take over: ask before discarding
      confirmDiscard().then(ok => { if (ok) dialog.close(); });
    }

    dialog.addEventListener('close', handleClose);
    dialog.addEventListener('cancel', handleCancel);
    return () => {
      dialog.removeEventListener('close', handleClose);
      dialog.removeEventListener('cancel', handleCancel);
      if (dialog.open) dialog.close();
    };
  }, [onClose]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const dialog = dialogRef.current;
    if (!dialog) return;
    if (open && !dialog.open) dialog.showModal();
    if (!open && dialog.open) dialog.close();
  }, [open]);

  return (
    <dialog ref={dialogRef} aria-labelledby={titleId}>
      <div className='panel'>{children}</div>
      <button type='button' onClick={() => dialogRef.current?.close()}>Close</button>
    </dialog>
  );
}
```

`showModal()` puts the dialog in the top layer, makes the rest of the document inert and exposes it as `aria-modal=true` implicitly, so the attribute is no longer a promise I keep by hand; Escape raises `cancel` and then `close`, and with several dialogs the browser dismisses only the last one shown. Two details to say out loud: `tabindex` must not go on `<dialog>` — it is not interactive and does not receive focus, so `tabIndex={-1}` was a div-only workaround — and initial placement is `autofocus` on the control the user needs first, or the dialog itself when the content starts with prose.

If the team cannot use native `<dialog>`, the hand-rolled version needs the four missing pieces: capture `document.activeElement` before moving focus in and restore it on close, with a stable fallback when the opener is gone; set `inert` on the app shell for as long as the overlay is open; cycle Tab at the boundaries from the live list of tabbable descendants, handling the empty case with the focusable container; and handle Escape at the document level with an ownership check so only the top-most dialog reacts. The accessible name stays `aria-labelledby`, the Close button stays visible, and the test is Escape pressed while focus is on a control inside the dialog.

