type Row = { id: string; label: string };
type Props = {
  rows: Row[];
  height: number;
  rowHeight: number;
  selectedId: string | null;
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
};

function VirtualList({ rows, height, rowHeight, selectedId, onSelect }: Props) {
  const [scrollTop, setScrollTop] = useState(0);
  const overscan = 4;                       // declared, never used
  const first = Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight);
  const count = Math.ceil(height / rowHeight);
  const windowRows = rows.slice(first, first + count);

  return (
    <div
      tabIndex={0}
      style={{ height, overflow: 'auto' }}
      onScroll={e => setScrollTop(e.currentTarget.scrollTop)}
    >
      <div style={{ height: rows.length * rowHeight, position: 'relative' }}>
        {windowRows.map((row, i) => (
          <div
            key={i}
            style={{ position: 'absolute', top: i * rowHeight, height: rowHeight }}
            onClick={() => onSelect(row.id)}
            aria-selected={row.id === selectedId}
          >
            {row.label}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

scenarios.modelAnswer:
Start with the math, because the first symptom is pure arithmetic. `scrollTop` says where the viewport starts; `first = floor(scrollTop / rowHeight)` is the first row in the window; the mounted rows are `rows[first .. first + count)`. Their offsets inside the spacer are therefore `(first + i) * rowHeight`. The code positions by the *window* index, so each render paints a block at offsets 0..count*rowHeight — the top of the spacer. At `scrollTop = 0` that coincides with the truth and the first screen looks perfect; as soon as the user scrolls, the painted block sits above the viewport and the visible area is empty spacer, which is why the scrollbar keeps moving while the list goes blank. Fixing that expression, or translating a window wrapper by `first * rowHeight`, restores the list.

Then the three contract gaps. `key={i}` binds identity to the slot, so when the window shifts by one row every mounted node is reused for a different row: per-row state, an in-flight transition or the focused element silently becomes another record. `aria-selected` on a plain div is ignored, because the attribute is only supported on `option`, `row`, `gridcell` and `tab`, and the list has no role at all. And the component scrolls nothing: there is no keyboard path, and no way to bring a selection set from outside into view.

The shape of the fix:

```tsx
const containerRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

function scrollToIndex(index: number) {
  const el = containerRef.current;
  if (!el) return;
  const top = index * rowHeight;
  if (top < el.scrollTop) el.scrollTop = top;
  else if (top + rowHeight > el.scrollTop + height) el.scrollTop = top + rowHeight - height;
}

const first = Math.max(0, Math.floor(scrollTop / rowHeight) - overscan);
const last = Math.min(rows.length, Math.ceil((scrollTop + height) / rowHeight) + overscan);
const windowRows = rows.slice(first, last);
```

`first` is clamped at 0 and `last` at the dataset length, so a filter that shrinks the data cannot produce an out-of-range window; the overscan now does the job it was declared for, one buffer row above and below so a fast flick does not show blank while React catches up; and the absolute offset stays `(first + i) * rowHeight`, which is the detail that breaks when someone adds overscan without adjusting the position.

Each row becomes `role=option` with `key={row.id}` and `aria-selected={row.id === selectedId}`, plus a roving tab stop: the selected row, or the first row when nothing is selected, has `tabIndex={0}` and the rest `-1`, so the list is one Tab stop. ArrowUp and ArrowDown move the active row and call `scrollToIndex` for the target, Enter and Space select, Home and End jump to the ends. `scrollToIndex` is why the outside-selection case works: the target row may not be mounted at all, so the list moves the container first and lets the render follow, instead of querying a node that does not exist. A polite live region saying 'Row 42 of 1,204' is the cheap way to keep a screen-reader user oriented.

The limit to state honestly: this contract only holds for fixed row heights. Variable heights need a measured offset cache and re-anchoring whenever a measurement changes, and virtualization still hides rows from find-in-page and from some browse-mode interactions, so a 'show all' path or a table view for small result sets is the fallback I would keep.

